---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { layers, localUrl } from "../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Architecture — OrbitKV"
  description="Engine-owned HBM, one Cache Manager per host, embedded catalogs and Mooncake transfers."
  active="Architecture"
>
  <div class="container interior">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Architecture / Ownership before placement</p>
      <h1>
        One cache API.
        <br />
        <span class="highlight">Across tiers.</span>
      </h1>
      <p>
        The engine owns GPU memory and model execution. OrbitKV owns external
        cache replicas, finds reusable prefixes, and holds pages until transfers
        finish.
      </p>
    </header>
    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="topology-title">
      <h2 id="topology-title">From one host to a shared cache</h2>
      <figure class="architecture-figure">
        <img
          src={localUrl("/architecture.svg")}
          width="1120"
          height="660"
          alt="Each inference host has an engine with its own HBM and an OrbitKV Cache Manager with DRAM, SSD and embedded catalog shards. UDS and iceoryx2 carry engine commands, CUDA IPC exposes GPU buffers, peer gRPC carries control, Mooncake transfers remote bytes, and etcd tracks membership and placement."
        />
        <figcaption>
          Single-node recovery is validated. The multi-node path is
          experimental; catalogs currently have one copy per shard. A shared API
          does not imply interchangeable KV layouts.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="layer-grid">
        <article class="layer">
          <span class="package-name">01 / Single node</span>
          <h3>Keep useful prefixes.</h3>
          <p>
            Run one manager beside an engine. DRAM and optional SSD extend its
            cache capacity. Standalone deployment needs neither etcd nor peer
            gRPC.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="layer">
          <span class="package-name">02 / Independent replicas</span>
          <h3>Reuse across workers.</h3>
          <p>
            Managers share compatible replicas through embedded catalogs. Cached
            candidates reduce discovery work; source authorization and Mooncake
            move the requested blocks. Cross-host serving is the next gate.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="layer">
          <span class="package-name">03 / Prefill &amp; decode</span>
          <h3>Compose the handoff.</h3>
          <p>
            A P/D connector transfers the current request. The cache retains KV
            for later requests. Combined serving qualification follows
            independent-replica sharing.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>How a request reuses KV</h2>
      <ol class="compile-flow">
        <li>
          <h3>Identify</h3>
          <p>
            The engine adapter supplies prefix identity and registered storage
            geometry.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Prepare</h3>
          <p>
            The manager finds DRAM, SSD or remote candidates within query byte
            budgets. SGLang validates complete prefix, window and checkpoint
            coverage before selecting a recovery boundary.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Restore</h3>
          <p>
            Leases retain source pages and destinations until transfer
            completion is known.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Continue</h3>
          <p>
            The engine resumes computation; completed KV can be published for
            later reuse.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="reading-copy">
        The vLLM adapter defers new admissions until an admitted restore reaches
        computation, avoiding deferred-queue capacity stalls. SGLang waits for
        leased readiness before allocating restore destinations. Each retains
        its own scheduler and HBM allocator.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href={localUrl("/docs/architecture/")}>
        Read the architecture and ownership contracts →
      </a>
    </section>
    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Implementation boundaries</h2>
      <div class="definition-list">
        {layers.map((layer) => (
          <div>
            <span class="package-name">{layer.name}</span>
            <p>{layer.detail}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>What comes next</h2>
      <p class="reading-copy">
        Qualify sustained single-node serving, then matching independent
        replicas, then P/D with reusable cache. Replicated catalog shards and
        controlled placement handoff precede production distributed deployment.
        General resharding, cross-engine byte reuse, complete recovery proofs,
        and KV-aware routing remain future work.
      </p>
      <div class="doc-links">
        <a href={localUrl("/docs/distributed-cache/")}>
          Distributed catalog design <span>→</span>
        </a>
        <a href={localUrl("/docs/state-identity/")}>
          Model-aware identity <span>→</span>
        </a>
        <a href={localUrl("/docs/distributed-comparison/")}>
          LMCache and Mooncake deployment paths <span>→</span>
        </a>
        <a href={localUrl("/docs/roadmap/")}>
          Roadmap and qualification gates <span>→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
